{
  "narrative_body": "Poland signed the largest single national allocation in the EU's new defence financing programme this week, becoming the first member state to ink a loan agreement under the Security Action for Europe instrument — securing up to €43.7 billion for military modernisation. The achievement came despite a presidential veto of the implementing legislation: the government bypassed it by invoking existing armed forces fund legal authority, proceeding on the theory that EU treaty law is directly binding without a domestic statute. The legal argument has not been tested in Polish courts. The government did not seek advance judicial validation. It signed anyway.\n\nHow Donald Tusk did it matters as much as what he did. By proceeding through a contractual channel rather than a statutory one, the government established — contested but operationally — that presidential vetoes cannot block EU treaty-based loan agreements. If courts uphold the theory, the scope of the veto narrows significantly across the entire category of EU-programme legislation. The veto's immediate cost was real: roughly PLN 7 billion previously earmarked for the border guard and police cannot be released. But Poland's primary defence modernisation financing track is now secured, with €43.7 billion on paper as its largest-ever such commitment.\n\nThe achievement arrived in the same week Washington made clear which Polish official it considers its primary interlocutor. The American president publicly suggested that some of the roughly 5,000 troops being withdrawn from Germany could relocate to Poland, calling Karol Nawrocki \"a great warrior\" and saying Poland would want them. Nawrocki — including during a visit to Lithuania — declared Poland ready to host. Tusk counselled caution, saying Poland should not \"poach",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Nawrocki appoints new BBN chief Grodecki after Cenckiewicz resignation, raising conflict-of-interest questions",
      "summary": "President Nawrocki named Bartosz Grodecki, a former Deputy Interior Minister, as the new head of the National Security Bureau, replacing Sławomir Cenckiewicz who resigned in April. The appointment drew immediate controversy because Grodecki had been an advisor to a defence-industry firm lobbying for legislation that Nawrocki himself had vetoed. Grodecki's inaugural statements signalled strong alignment with the presidency against the Tusk government, including on SAFE and the relocation of US troops."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trump suggests US troops from Germany could relocate to Poland; Nawrocki welcomes it, Tusk more cautious",
      "summary": "US President Donald Trump publicly suggested that some of the ~5,000 US troops being withdrawn from Germany could be redeployed to Poland, praising Nawrocki as 'a great warrior' and saying 'Poland would want this.' President Nawrocki — including during a separate visit to Lithuania — enthusiastically declared Poland ready to host the troops. Prime Minister Tusk struck a more cautious tone, saying Poland should not 'poach' soldiers from allies, which drew criticism from Kaczyński and the opposition. New BBN chief Grodecki called Trump's statement 'absolutely breakthrough.'"
    },
    {
      "headline": "193rd Smolensk monthly commemoration disrupted by counter-protesters; Kaczyński threatens future prosecutions",
      "summary": "The 193rd monthly Smolensk commemoration took place in Warsaw's Piłsudski Square with Jarosław Kaczyński and a delegation of PiS politicians. The ceremony was again disrupted by counter-demonstrators, prompting Kaczyński to accuse them of being 'Russian agents' and to warn that 'honest courts will resolve this and these people will be somewhere else' — remarks interpreted as threats of future prosecution. A number of senior PiS figures were absent."
    }
  ]
